Yes. In an Nginx `upstream` block, `server` can be an IP address or a hostname/FQDN, so `s1.domain.com` or `something.domain.com` is valid.

Example:

```nginx
upstream myCloud {
    server s1.domain.com;
    server 10.0.1.2;
    server 10.0.1.3;
}
```

A few notes:
- Make sure the hostname resolves correctly via DNS.
- Nginx resolves hostnames at startup/reload unless you use a resolver in certain dynamic setups.
- You can also mix hostnames and IPs in the same upstream.

If you want, I can also show a full working Nginx load-balancing config.

In Nginx, `location` blocks must be inside a `server` block, not directly under `http`.

If you want to avoid repeating it for every site, put the `location` rule in a separate file and `include` it inside each `server` block, like this:

`includes/gfx-expires-header.conf`
```nginx
location ~* \.(png|jpg|jpeg|gif|ico)$ {
    expires 1y;
}
```

Then in each `server` block:

```nginx
include includes/gfx-expires-header.conf;
```

So the server location is: inside each `server` block via an included file.

Use `glob()` to get the files in the user’s directory, then print them in table rows.

Example:

```php
<?php
$userDir = "uploads/" . $_SESSION['user_id'] . "/";

echo "<table>";
foreach (glob($userDir . "*") as $file) {
    if (is_file($file)) {
        echo "<tr><td>" . htmlspecialchars(basename($file)) . "</td>";
        echo "<td>" . filesize($file) . "</td></tr>";
    }
}
echo "</table>";
?>
```

`glob()` lists matching files, `is_file()` filters out folders, and `basename()` shows just the filename. Make sure each user has a separate directory and you sanitize any user input.

Yes — Google Analytics will be more readable if you pass a descriptive product name in `name` and keep `sku` as the actual code. For example:

ga('ecommerce:addItem', {
  id: '1234',
  name: 'Product name colour size',
  sku: 'DD23444',
  category: '...',
  price: '11.99',
  quantity: '1'
});

As for eCommerce platforms with a good product configurator, it depends on your needs, but common strong options are Magento/Adobe Commerce, Shopify with apps, WooCommerce with configurator plugins, and PrestaShop modules. The best choice is usually the one whose module lets you expose the product attributes you want in tracking.

`#menu_bar` and `#content_area` are block elements, so they stack vertically by default. Put them side by side with `float` (or flexbox).

Try:

```css
#menu_bar{
  float: left;
  width: 20%;
}

#content_area{
  float: left;
  width: 75%;
  margin-left: 2%;
}
```

Also clear the float after them:

```css
#footer{
  clear: both;
}
```

If you want a cleaner modern layout, use `display: flex;` on their parent container instead.

Yes—use one value and concatenate the categories into a single custom variable, separated by a delimiter.

Example:
_gaq.push('_setCustomVar', 1, 'categories', '|catA|catB|catC|', 3);
_gaq.push('_trackPageview');

This avoids sending multiple pageviews/events. It’s not a perfect multi-value field, but you can still search/filter for individual categories in the Analytics UI by matching the delimiter pattern.

Google/PageSpeed Insights doesn’t use a real phone screen; it emulates mobile in a WebKit-based renderer. So `display:none` in a CSS media query is handled normally based on the emulated viewport width, not on Google “reacting” specially.

If an element is hidden with:

```css
@media (max-width: 479px) {
  .myclass { display: none; }
}
```

then it will be hidden whenever the viewport matches that query, including in Google’s mobile preview.

Tip: make sure you use the viewport meta tag:

```html
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
```

That helps the emulation and mobile browsers interpret your media queries correctly.

Yes — for a one-page site, you can hide all sections and show only the section matched by the clicked jump link.

A simple pattern is:
- give each section an `id`
- on click, hide all sections with `display:none`
- show only the target section
- optionally update the URL hash so back/forward works

Example:

```html
<nav>
  <a href="#about" class="jump">About</a>
  <a href="#contact" class="jump">Contact</a>
</nav>

<section id="about" class="page-section">About content</section>
<section id="contact" class="page-section" style="display:none;">Contact content</section>
```

```js
document.querySelectorAll('.jump').forEach(link => {
  link.addEventListener('click', e => {
    e.preventDefault();
    document.querySelectorAll('.page-section').forEach(s => s.style.display = 'none');
    document.querySelector(link.getAttribute('href')).style.display = 'block';
  });
});
```

